------------ Task 1 ------------
Prompt for task Task 1:
Consider the following story:

Story 1: William was a patient in a psychiatric hospital who was confined indoors almost all the time. He could never pass the monthly room inspections so he hated them. He spent most of his time daydreaming about food. A few days before the April inspection William’s room was still a mess since he had done nothing but daydream. To provide William with an incentive, the nurse promised him some gingerbread from the cookie shop if he scrubbed his room and put it in order once and for all. William was overjoyed. But there was no longer enough time for him to put it in order. As a result, he did not pass the inspection and did not get any gingerbread. William sulked all day and slammed his door so hard the plaster cracked, but he still didn’t get any gingerbread.

Now consider two more stories:

Story A: Karen disliked high school so she always had trouble getting passing grades in her classes. Vacations were her favorite part of the school year. Not long before the end of her fourth year of school, Karen was not doing well at all because she despised school. To motivate her, Karen’s father promised her a trip to Hawaii if she would just pass enough of her classes to graduate. This made Karen extremely happy. During the last few weeks of school she spent most of her time dreaming about Hawaii and preparing for her trip there. Consequently, she failed to graduate and she did not get to go.

Story B: Karen loved vacations and especially wanted to go to Hawaii.  But she wasn’t a good student, disliked school, and was failing because she was distracted with constant thoughts about traveling to Hawaii.  Karen’s father made her feel better by telling her that he would pay for a trip to Hawaii if she got better grades before she graduated from high school in a few weeks.  But she didn’t have enough time to bring up her grades before graduation, so didn’t get the opportunity to go to Hawaii.

Which of Story A and Story B is a better analogy to Story 1? Is the best answer Story A, Story B, or both are equally analogous?

The following generation flags are not valid and may be ignored: ['temperature', 'top_k']. Set `TRANSFORMERS_VERBOSITY=info` for more details.
Full Qwen output (25.70 seconds): The best answer is: **Story A is a better analogy to Story 1**.

### Here's why:

**Story 1** involves:
- **William**, a patient in a psychiatric hospital who is **confined indoors** and **daydreams about food**.
- He is **promised a reward (gingerbread)** if he **cleans his room**.
- He becomes **overjoyed** at the promise, but **fails to act** because he **daydreams** instead of cleaning.
- As a result, he **fails the inspection** and **does not get the reward**.

This is a **classic case of a reward being offered as motivation, but the person becomes so focused on the reward (daydreaming about it) that they fail to take the necessary action to earn it**.

---

### Now, let's compare with **Story A** and **Story B**:

#### **Story A**:
- **Karen** dislikes school and is failing.
- Her **father promises her a trip to Hawaii** if she passes enough classes to graduate.
- Karen becomes **extremely happy** and **dreams about Hawaii**.
- She **fails to graduate** and **does not get the trip**.

This mirrors **Story 1** closely:
- The **reward (trip to Hawaii)** is **promised as an incentive**.
- The **subject (Karen)** becomes **overjoyed** and **daydreams about the reward**.
- As a result, she **fails to take the necessary action (passing classes)** and **does not get the reward**.

This is a **direct analogy** to **Story 1** in terms of **structure, motivation, and outcome**.

---

#### **Story B**:
- **Karen** loves vacations and wants to go to Hawaii.
- She is **not a good student**, **fails classes**, and is **distracted by thoughts of Hawaii**.
- Her **father offers to pay for the trip** if she **improves her grades**.
- She **doesn’t have enough time** to improve her grades and **doesn’t get the trip**.

This is **similar**, but **less analogous** to **Story 1** for a few reasons:
- In **Story B**, Karen is **already distracted by Hawaii**, not **motivated by a promise** of it.
- The **father’s promise** is not the **main motivator** in the same way as in **Story 1**.
- The **failure to act** is more due to **lack of time** than **daydreaming about the reward**.

---

### Conclusion:

**Story A** is a **better analogy** to **Story 1** because it mirrors the **structure of a reward being offered as motivation, the subject becoming overly focused on the reward, and failing to take the necessary action to earn it**.

**Answer: Story A**.
------------ Task 2 ------------
Prompt for task Task 2:
Consider the following story:

Story 1: Mr. Newton was the manager of a company that made razors. One year, an inventor in his company perfected a metal that would stay sharper than any previous razor. However, not long after that, Mr. Newton was using one of these new razors and he cut his neck rather severely. As a result of this incident the inventor recommended a safety feature that would protect people from injuring themselves. After what had happened, Mr. Newton considered the safety feature absolutely necessary. The incident had alarmed him so much that he began to produce the safety razor right away. Luckily, his neck did not get infected so everything was alright.

Now consider two more stories:

Story A: Ms. Boyce was in charge of installing sun lamps sold by Smith Tan Company. In 1980, a supplier for the company perfected a filament that could produce more intense heat than any previous filament. Because it was so hot, the supplier recommended a warning label that would protect people from hurting themselves with the new lamp. But Mrs. Boyce considered the labels unnecessary. It would be too much trouble to make them. But later, when she was using one of these new heat lamps herself, she was seriously burned. After this accident she was so frightened that she had the warning labels printed immediately.

Story B: The Smith Tan Company sold sun lamps, with Ms. Boyce leading the firm’s installation of these lamps. One of the firm’s suppliers created a novel filament for these lamps; this filament generated more heat than past and current filaments.  Ms. Boyce got a bad burn when trying out one of these lamps.  She reported this to the firm, and the supplier proposed putting a label on the lamps, warning of the possibility of burns.  Ms. Boyce was convinced by her burn to get the company to quickly go ahead with the labels.

Which of Story A and Story B is a better analogy to Story 1? Is the best answer Story A, Story B, or both are equally analogous?

Full Qwen output (31.75 seconds): The best answer is: **Story B** is a better analogy to **Story 1**.

### Here's why:

Let's break down the key elements of **Story 1** and compare them to **Story A** and **Story B**.

---

### **Key Elements of Story 1:**

1. **Mr. Newton** is the **manager** of a company that makes **razors**.
2. An **inventor** in the company develops a **new material** (a metal that stays sharper).
3. Mr. Newton **uses the new razor** and **injures himself** (cuts his neck).
4. The **inventor recommends a safety feature** (to prevent injury).
5. Mr. Newton, **alarmed by the incident**, **agrees to implement the safety feature**.
6. The **incident leads to a change in policy** (safety feature is added).
7. The **injury is serious**, but **no infection occurs**, so the outcome is **not fatal**.

---

### **Story A:**

1. **Ms. Boyce** is in charge of **installing sun lamps**.
2. A **supplier** develops a **new filament** that is **hotter**.
3. The **supplier recommends a warning label**.
4. **Ms. Boyce considers the labels unnecessary** (she thinks it's too much trouble).
5. She **gets burned** using the new lamp.
6. After the **burn**, she **agrees to put on the warning labels**.

**Comparison:**
- The **manager (Ms. Boyce)** **initially ignores** the **safety recommendation**.
- The **safety feature** (warning label) is **recommended by the supplier**, not the manager.
- The **manager is injured** and then **changes her mind**.
- This is **similar** to Story 1, but with a **key difference**: in Story 1, the **manager (Mr. Newton)** **initially accepts** the **safety feature** **after being injured**, not **before**.

---

### **Story B:**

1. **Ms. Boyce** is in charge of **installing sun lamps**.
2. A **supplier** develops a **new filament** that is **hotter**.
3. **Ms. Boyce gets burned** using the new lamp.
4. She **reports the incident** to the firm.
5. The **supplier recommends a warning label**.
6. **Ms. Boyce is convinced by the burn** to **implement the warning label**.

**Comparison:**
- The **manager (Ms. Boyce)** is **injured**.
- The **safety feature** (warning label) is **recommended by the supplier** after the incident.
- The **manager is convinced by the incident** to **implement the safety feature**.
- This **closely mirrors** the **sequence of events** in **Story 1**:
  - **Innovation** (new material/filament).
  - **Injury** caused by the new product.
  - **Recommendation for safety feature**.
  - **Manager accepts the recommendation** after being **alarmed by the incident**.

---

### **Conclusion:**

- **Story A** is **similar**, but the **manager initially rejects** the safety feature, which is **not the case in Story 1**.
- **Story B** **matches Story 1 more closely** in terms of **sequence of events**, **manager's response**, and **reasoning**.

### ✅ **Final Answer: Story B is a better analogy to Story 1.**
------------ Task 3 ------------
Prompt for task Task 3:
Consider the following story:

Story 1: Joseph was a millionaire who hired a chauffeur to drive his Rolls-Royce. He used to brag to his wife that he would never be late for his conferences since he had hired a chauffeur. One morning when he was in a great hurry, he went to find the chauffeur. But he was asleep. He thought his services would not be needed that day. Thus Joseph was very late for his conference after all. To make sure this would not happen again, Joseph hired a second chauffeur. This turned out to be very expensive because the second chauffeur wanted a fancy uniform as well as a high salary.

Now consider two more stories:

Story A: Many years ago there was a man named Alexander who had two wives living in two houses: He liked to boast to a friend that with his two wives he would always eat well. One day when he was very hungry, Alexander went to his first wife. But she had only prepared enough for herself and their baby. She thought Alexander would eat with his other wife that day. Alexander then went to his second wife, but found she had already finished. She saw him go to the other house so she thought he was eating there. Thus Alexander went without any dinner at all.

Story B: A man called Alexander and his wife lived in a big house. Alexander’s friend often heard Alexander vaunt the fact that his wife cooked amazing food that he loved to eat. One day the wife expected her husband would be away, so she cooked a small amount of food for the baby and herself.  When Alexander came home there was no food left.  Disappointed and wanting to make sure to avoid hunger, Alexander got married to another woman in addition to his original wife.

Which of Story A and Story B is a better analogy to Story 1? Is the best answer Story A, Story B, or both are equally analogous?

Full Qwen output (28.20 seconds): The best answer is: **Story A** is a better analogy to **Story 1**.

### Here's why:

**Story 1** involves **Joseph**, who hires a **chauffeur** to avoid being late. However, when the chauffeur is absent, Joseph is late. To prevent this, he hires a **second chauffeur**, which ends up being **costly** and **inefficient**.

This story illustrates a **flawed solution to a problem** — Joseph assumes that **adding more of the same resource** (a second chauffeur) will solve the problem of reliability, but it doesn't address the **root cause** (the chauffeur might not be available). It also shows that **the solution is more expensive and less effective** than expected.

---

### Now, let's compare with **Story A** and **Story B**:

#### **Story A**:
- **Alexander** has **two wives** and **boasts** that he will always eat well.
- One day, he goes to the **first wife**, but she assumes he will eat with the **second wife**.
- He goes to the **second wife**, who assumes he is with the **first wife**.
- **Result**: He eats **nothing**.
- He **adds another wife** to avoid this problem, but the **problem persists**.

This is **structurally very similar** to Story 1:
- **Problem**: Reliability of a resource (a chauffeur, or a wife who will cook).
- **Solution**: Adding **another resource** (a second chauffeur, a second wife).
- **Outcome**: The **problem is not solved**, and the **solution is costly or inefficient**.

#### **Story B**:
- **Alexander** has **one wife** who cooks well.
- One day, she **doesn't cook enough** for him.
- He **gets a second wife** to ensure he always has food.
- **Result**: He now has **two wives**, but the **problem is not clearly addressed**.

This is **less analogous** to Story 1 for a few reasons:
- The **problem** in Story B is **not about reliability** of the resource (the wife), but about **quantity** of food.
- The **solution** (adding a second wife) is **not clearly a flawed or inefficient** one — it's more of a **precautionary measure**.
- The **outcome** is not clearly **inefficient or costly** in the same way as in Story 1.

---

### Conclusion:

**Story A** is a **better analogy** to **Story 1** because:
- Both involve **relying on a single resource** that may not be available.
- Both involve **adding a second resource** to solve the problem.
- Both show that **the solution doesn't work as intended**, and **the problem persists**.
- Both highlight the **flawed logic** of assuming that **more of the same** will solve the problem.

**Story B**, while similar in some aspects, is **less analogous** because it focuses more on **quantity** than **reliability**, and the **solution is not clearly flawed**.

### Final Answer: **Story A** is a better analogy to Story 1.
------------ Task 4 ------------
Prompt for task Task 4:
Consider the following story:

Story 1: Karla, an old hawk, lived at the top of a tall oak tree.  One afternoon, she saw a hunter on the ground with a bow and some crude arrows that had no feathers.  The hunter took aim and shot at the hawk but missed.  Karla knew the hunter wanted her feathers so she glided down to the hunter and offered to give him a few.  The hunter was so grateful that he pledged never to shoot at a hawk again.  He went off and shot deer instead.

Now consider two more stories:

Story A: Once there was a small country called Zerdia that learned to make the world’s smartest computer.  Zerdia sold one of its supercomputers to its neighbor, Gagrach, so Gagrach would promise never to attack Zerdia. But one day Zerdia was overwhelmed by a surprise attack from Gagrach.  As it capitulated, the crippled government of Zerdia realized that the attacker’s missiles had been guided by Zerdian supercomputers.

Story B: Once there was a small country called Zerdia that learned to make the world’s smartest computer.  Another country called Gagrach declared war on Zerdia and launched a missile attack that didn’t work because the missiles took a wrong trajectory.  The small country, Zerdia, had the smart computers that they assumed Gagrach was after, and Zerdia offered Gagrach a deal that pleased the Gagrach government enough to prevent new attacks.  The deal was to sell Gagrach a set of these machines.

Which of Story A and Story B is a better analogy to Story 1? Is the best answer Story A, Story B, or both are equally analogous?

Full Qwen output (23.07 seconds): The best answer is: **Story B** is a better analogy to **Story 1**.

### Here's why:

In **Story 1**, we have a **moral dilemma** and a **sacrificial act**. Karla, the hawk, **knows** the hunter wants her feathers, and instead of fleeing or fighting, she **offers** him what he wants in order to **prevent harm** to herself and others. The hunter, moved by her gesture, **changes his behavior** and stops hunting hawks, choosing instead to hunt deer. The key elements are:

- **Anticipation of harm** (the hunter wants to shoot her).
- **Voluntary sacrifice** (she offers her feathers).
- **Change in behavior** (the hunter stops hunting hawks).
- **Moral resolution** (the hunter is convinced not to harm hawks).

---

### Now, let's compare with **Story A** and **Story B**:

#### **Story A**:
- Zerdia sells a supercomputer to Gagrach in exchange for a promise of peace.
- Gagrach **breaks the promise** and attacks Zerdia.
- The **supercomputer is used against Zerdia** by Gagrach.

This is **not analogous** to Story 1 because:
- There is **no voluntary sacrifice** or **moral gesture** from Zerdia.
- The **trust is broken**, and the **outcome is betrayal**, not a positive change in behavior.
- The **analogy breaks down** because the outcome is **negative**, not the result of a moral or selfless act.

#### **Story B**:
- Zerdia has a supercomputer.
- Gagrach attacks, but the attack **fails** (missiles take wrong trajectory).
- Zerdia **offers** the supercomputer to Gagrach in a **deal**.
- Gagrach is **pleased** and **prevents new attacks**.

This is **analogous** to Story 1 because:
- Zerdia **anticipates** that Gagrach wants the supercomputer.
- Zerdia **offers** the supercomputer (like Karla offering her feathers).
- Gagrach **changes behavior** (stops attacking).
- The **outcome is positive** and **moral**, similar to the resolution in Story 1.

---

### Conclusion:

**Story B** is the **better analogy** to **Story 1** because both involve a **voluntary act of giving** to **prevent harm**, and both result in a **positive change in the other party's behavior**. **Story A** involves betrayal and is not analogous in structure or moral tone.
------------ Task 5 ------------
Prompt for task Task 5:
Consider the following story:

Story 1: Peter was a young man from Iceland who moved to Florida one summer. He was very self-conscious about his fair skin. Peter thought that people would not accept him unless he tanned himself golden-brown like everyone else. So Peter spent a whole day in the sun, trying to get a tan. He didn’t understand how dangerous the sun could be. Consequently, by evening he had second degree burns over most of his body and he had to go to the hospital. Peter decided that Florida was not for him and booked the next ocean liner back to Iceland. He spent the whole trip under a large umbrella keeping cool.

Now consider two more stories:

Story A: Quiggly Company was a small company that manufactured conventional electrical components. They had just started producing integrated circuits and decided that the real action in the integrated circuit market was in space manufacturing. But the market analysts gave them a stern warning. They said that space factories were too expensive and that the market was too competitive. If Quiggly went into space manufacturing, the analysts predicted they would lose everything and their stock would plunge. Quiggly Company was so annoyed at having to play it safe when everyone else had a chance to make a killing that they decided to go back to manufacturing conventional electric components.

Story B: Quiggly Company was a small company that manufactured conventional electrical components. One year, Quiggly Co. began to get into the integrated circuit market. However, Quiggly Company felt that to be competitive it would have to have really sophisticated space factories, as did all the other manufacturers of integrated circuits. Quiggly decided to put all its assets into having a space factory. However, contrary to Quiggly’s knowledge, space manufacturing was a financial risk.  It turned out that within six months, Quiggly Company had spent much more than they could afford and their stocks plunged to an all time low. The company made a judgment call.  They pulled out of integrated circuits altogether in favor of their original business: making conventional electrical components.

Which of Story A and Story B is a better analogy to Story 1? Is the best answer Story A, Story B, or both are equally analogous?

Full Qwen output (24.42 seconds): The best answer is: **Story B** is a better analogy to **Story 1**.

### Here's why:

**Story 1** is about **Peter**, who makes a **poor decision based on a misunderstanding of risk and consequences**. He believes that tanning is necessary to fit in, and he ignores the dangers of sun exposure. As a result, he suffers serious harm and then retreats from the situation he was in (Florida) to return to his original environment (Iceland).

This story illustrates:
- A **misguided belief** about what is necessary to fit in or succeed.
- A **lack of awareness of the risks** involved in the action.
- A **negative consequence** that leads to a **reversal of course** (returning to the original situation).

---

### Comparing to **Story A**:

In **Story A**, Quiggly Company is warned by **market analysts** that space manufacturing is too risky and expensive. Despite this, they **choose to go back to their original business** (conventional components) because they are **annoyed** at having to play it safe.

This is **not analogous** to Story 1 because:
- The decision to retreat is **not based on a misunderstanding or a miscalculation**, but rather on **annoyance** at being cautious.
- There is **no negative consequence** that forces the reversal — the company simply chooses to avoid risk.
- The **analysts’ warnings** are **external and accurate**, not based on the company’s own flawed understanding.

---

### Comparing to **Story B**:

In **Story B**, Quiggly Company **decides to enter a risky market (space manufacturing)**, **believing it is necessary to be competitive**, and **ignores the financial risks**. They **invest heavily**, **suffer a financial loss**, and **then retreat** to their original business.

This is **analogous to Story 1** because:
- The company, like Peter, **believes that a certain action (space manufacturing, tanning) is necessary to be accepted or competitive**.
- They **underestimate the risks** involved.
- They **suffer a negative consequence** (financial loss, burns).
- They **retreat from the situation** and return to their original path (conventional components, Iceland).

---

### Conclusion:

**Story B** is the **better analogy** to **Story 1** because both involve:
- A **misguided belief** in the necessity of a risky action.
- A **lack of awareness of the risks**.
- A **negative consequence**.
- A **reversal of course** back to the original path.

**Story A** is not as strong an analogy because it lacks the **misguided belief and negative consequence** that are central to Story 1.
------------ Task 6 ------------
Prompt for task Task 6:
Consider the following story:

Story 1: Julius was a mule who discovered several pears sitting in a window sill.  He thought to himself, “These pears seem to be rotten.  Perhaps I’ll get some and find out if my prediction is correct.” However, the pears were too high for Julius.  And because he was hungry he felt too weak to jump up to them.  Naturally, this was rather disappointing.  He closed his eyes and went to sleep, dreaming about mountains of pears.

Now consider two more stories:

Story A: A girl named Cindy found some records she wanted at a record store.  She really wanted them but, needless to say, they were too expensive for her.  After brooding for a while, Cindy got over her disappointment. “These records are probably boring,” she thought.  “So even if I could afford them I wouldn’t listen to them.”

Story B: A girl named Cindy glimpsed some records she was curious about at a record store.   Cindy didn’t think the records would be fun to listen to but wanted to purchase them to see whether they were or not.  Sadly, she saw there was no way she could afford them.

Which of Story A and Story B is a better analogy to Story 1? Is the best answer Story A, Story B, or both are equally analogous?

Full Qwen output (28.09 seconds): The best answer is: **Story B** is a better analogy to **Story 1**.

### Here's why:

Let’s break down the key elements of **Story 1** and compare them to **Story A** and **Story B**.

---

### **Story 1 (Julius the mule):**
- **Desire**: Julius wants to get the pears.
- **Observation**: He thinks they might be rotten, but he wants to check.
- **Obstacle**: The pears are too high for him to reach.
- **Emotion**: He is hungry and weak, so he can't jump up.
- **Outcome**: He gives up and dreams about pears.

This is a story of **unfulfilled desire** due to **physical limitation**, but with a **curious and somewhat rational mindset**. Julius is not just disappointed — he is **disappointed in a way that reflects his own reasoning** (he thought they might be rotten, but still wanted to check). His disappointment is **real**, but he **doesn’t completely reject the value** of the pears — he just can’t reach them.

---

### **Story A (Cindy and the records):**
- **Desire**: Cindy wants the records.
- **Observation**: She thinks they might be boring.
- **Obstacle**: They are too expensive.
- **Emotion**: She broods, then **concludes** that even if she could afford them, she wouldn’t want them.
- **Outcome**: She **rejects the value** of the records.

In this case, Cindy **changes her mind** about the records — she **no longer wants them**. This is a **change in desire**, not just an unfulfilled one. She **concludes** that the records are not worth her time or money, even if she could afford them.

This is **not** analogous to Julius, who **still wanted** the pears but **couldn’t reach them**.

---

### **Story B (Cindy and the records):**
- **Desire**: Cindy is curious about the records.
- **Observation**: She doesn’t think they’ll be fun, but she **wants to purchase them to see**.
- **Obstacle**: She can’t afford them.
- **Emotion**: She is **sad** but doesn’t reject the records.
- **Outcome**: She **doesn’t give up on the idea** of the records — she just can’t get them.

This is **very similar** to Julius. Both:
- Want something (pears / records).
- Are **curious or interested** in them, even if they have doubts.
- Are **unable to obtain** them due to an **external obstacle** (height / money).
- **Don’t reject the value** of the object — they just can’t get it.

---

### **Conclusion:**
- **Story A** is **not** a good analogy because Cindy **changes her mind** about the records.
- **Story B** is a **better analogy** because Cindy **still wants to see** if the records are worth it — just like Julius wants to see if the pears are rotten.

### ✅ **Answer: Story B is a better analogy to Story 1.**
------------ Task 7 ------------
Prompt for task Task 7:
Consider the following story:

Story 1: Percy the mockingbird spent the whole warm season chirping and twittering.  When it began to get colder Percy visited a squirrel and sang a song for her, expecting to get some of the squirrel’s sunflower seeds in return.  However, the squirrel was very disappointed in him. “You are a terrible singer!” she yelled.  “I’m not giving you any of my wheat.” A tear  rolled down Percy’s cheek, and he vowed to give up singing for good.

Now consider two more stories:

Story A: Sam traveled all over the world buying beautiful things.  When he ran out of money he paid a visit to his mother.  However, she was not at all pleased with him. “While I have been hard at work you have been wasting your time,” she said.  Sam gave her a gift he bought in Tibet, hoping she would give him a loan in return.  But she was still not pleased.  “I will not give you any of my hard-earned money!” she exclaimed.

Story B: Sam traveled all over the world buying beautiful things.  At some point he had no more money left.  He went home to see his mother.  He wanted to get a loan from her, so he presented her with a gift for her that he bought while in Tibet, hoping she would reciprocate with money.  His angry mother responded that the Tibetan gift was not worth anything.  “I will not give you any of my hard-earned money!” she exclaimed.

Which of Story A and Story B is a better analogy to Story 1? Is the best answer Story A, Story B, or both are equally analogous?

Full Qwen output (24.70 seconds): The best answer is: **Story B** is a better analogy to **Story 1**.

### Here's why:

In **Story 1**, the key elements are:

- **Percy** (the mockingbird) **offers a song** (a gift or favor) to **the squirrel** in **expectation of a return** (sunflower seeds).
- The squirrel **rejects the gift** and **refuses to reciprocate**.
- Percy is **disappointed** and **gives up** on singing.

This is a **clear case of an expected reciprocal relationship** being **disrupted**, leading to **emotional fallout**.

