Story B: Zebediah, the old miser, was sitting on his back porch counting his money. Zebediah was startled and scared when he saw the forsythia bushes move and a beggar emerge from them.  “Go away,” said Zebediah loudly.  This made the beggar look at him and start to make his way to the old miser.  “I shouldn’t have said anything,” Zebediah thought, “and I need to run away,” as he threw a bag of coins to the beggar.  The beggar grabbed for the bag, and Zebediah took the opportunity to run into the house and lock the door, hugging his last few bags of money.  Zebediah was able to fall asleep, since he figured that the beggar wouldn’t be able to get in.  But later, after getting up, he counted his bags again and one was gone, even though the door was still locked.

Which of Story A and Story B is a better analogy to Story 1? Is the best answer Story A, Story B, or both are equally analogous?

